# 15. Successful Recovery Is Not a Single Model

Recent cases illustrate several different recovery pathways.

### Habitat conservation

San Benito evening-primrose increased from nine known occurrences when listed in 1985 to 79 by the time recovery was determined. FWS attributes the improvement to research and habitat conservation involving BLM and partners.

### Multi-jurisdictional aquatic recovery

The razorback sucker was downlisted in 2026 after more than three decades of cooperative work involving federal, state, Tribal, regional, private, and nonprofit organizations. Its largest population reportedly increased from roughly 100 wild fish in the late 1990s to approximately 36,000 adults.

### Habitat and population management

The recent aeʻo downlisting involved the State of Hawaiʻi, military installations, private landowners, and local organizations, illustrating the importance of conservation beyond federal land.

### Large population increase

Rough popcornflower increased from roughly 7,000 plants in eight populations at listing to more than two million plants across 18 populations before its 2026 downlisting.

These cases should eventually become comparative case studies rather than merely examples in narrative prose.

